a. health;

b. education;

c. legal aid;

d. assistance;

e. social, mental and spiritual guidance;

f. social rehabilitation; and/or

g. reintegration.

Article 39

Provision of services for families as referred to in Article 34 paragraph (1) letter b includes:

a. communication, information and education;

b. contraceptive devices and medicines;

c. recording and reporting of family planning services;

d. adequate health service facilities and infrastructure; and

e. lactation rooms in every public facility.

Article 49

(1) Promotive efforts for basic health services as referred to in Article 48 paragraph (2) letter a are aimed at improving health status in a more optimal manner.

(2) Promotive efforts as referred to in paragraph (1) are carried out by providing socialization or outreach to the community regarding healthy lifestyles which include:

a. the importance of exclusive breastfeeding for infants;

b. the importance of immunization for toddlers;

c. clean living patterns;

d. the importance of nutritious food; and

e. the dangers of drugs and smoking.

(3) In addition to promotive efforts as referred to in paragraph (2), the City Government may carry out other activities in the context of health implementation.

Article 50

(1) Preventive efforts for basic health services as referred to in Article 48 paragraph (2) letter a are aimed at preventing the occurrence of disease or health problems.

(2) Efforts as referred to in paragraph (1) are carried out by:

a. requiring mothers to provide exclusive breastfeeding to infants up to 6 (six) months of age;

b. providing free immunization and vitamins to infants and toddlers;

c. providing nutritious food to toddlers from underprivileged families;

d. optimizing the role of posyandu for health services for mothers and toddlers;

e. conducting periodic health examinations; and

f. providing sanitation facilities and clean water in every school.

Article 19

Forms of integrated services for women and children victims of acts of Violence include:

a. complaint handling;

b. health rehabilitation;

c. social rehabilitation;

d. victim assistance, enforcement and legal aid; and

e. repatriation and social reintegration.

Article 41

(1) In addition to the City Government, private parties providing Public services are required to provide lactation rooms.

(2) Lactation rooms as referred to in paragraph (1) constitute proper and adequate rooms.

(3) Private parties that do not provide lactation rooms as referred to in paragraph (2) shall be subject to administrative sanctions in the form of:

a. written warning;

b. limitation of business activities;

c. suspension of business activities;

d. administrative fines; and/or

e. revocation of permit.

Part Four Social Rehabilitation

Article 23

(1) Social rehabilitation as referred to in Article 19 letter c is intended to restore and develop the capacity of women and children victims of acts of Violence who experience social dysfunction to be able to carry out their social functions in society properly.

(2) Social rehabilitation as referred to in paragraph (1) includes services:

a. motivation and psychosocial diagnosis;

b. care and nurturing;

c. vocational training and entrepreneurship development;

d. spiritual mental guidance;

e. physical guidance;

f. social guidance and psychosocial counseling;

g. accessibility services;

h. social assistance and aid;

i. resocialization guidance;

j. follow-up guidance; and/or

k. referral.

(3) The provision of services as referred to in paragraph (2) is organized by:

a. Regional Apparatus that has duties and functions in the social field;

b. Regional Apparatus in charge of Women's and Children's Empowerment, and may cooperate with integrated service centers, women crisis centers or social community organizations that already exist; or c. the ministry of religion.

Article 24

(1) Counseling services as referred to in Article 23 paragraph (2) letter a aim to:

a. assist women and children victims of acts of violence in recognizing their problems and finding effective ways to overcome them themselves;

b. empower women and children victims of acts of Violence to determine their future;

c. provide moral support to women and children victims of acts of Violence in facing the process they are undergoing; and

d. make women and children victims of acts of Violence feel accepted in the community environment.

(2) Counseling services are carried out by social rehabilitation officers who are gender responsive, consisting of:

a. counselors;

b. psychologists; and

c. psychiatrists.

(3) Counseling services are carried out in a place that guarantees a sense of security, comfort and confidentiality of information from women and children victims of acts of Violence.

(4) The stages of counseling services include:

a. initial counseling;

b. follow-up counseling;

c. case discussion; and

d. site visits.

Part Six

Enhancement of the Role, Quality, and Capability of Women in the Social Sector

Article 14

Efforts to enhance the role, quality and capability of women in the social sector include:

a. enhancement of knowledge and skills to encourage the fulfillment of education in accordance with existing potential;

b. enhancement and fulfillment of health quality for women;

c. enhancement of awareness and knowledge about independent, healthy and prosperous family planning including access to consultation services and marriage registration;

d. enhancement of women's awareness of the impact of early marriage;

e. facilitating and endeavoring the preservation of customs and the development of science, technology, arts and culture for the advancement of women;

f. conducting enhancement of women's awareness and knowledge regarding independent, healthy and prosperous family planning;

g. conducting programs to reduce maternal mortality rates; and

h. encouraging the establishment of women's organizations in the City.

Article 28

(1) Law enforcement and legal aid services are intended to provide assistance and Protection to women and children victims of acts of Violence and witnesses with a gender responsive approach so that victims obtain justice and legal certainty.

(2) Types of legal aid services include:

a. legal aid in the reporting process and preparation of investigation reports;

b. legal aid in the prosecution process at the prosecutor's office;

c. legal aid in the examination process at court;

d. legal aid in the court hearing decision process until there is a final legal decision; and

e. legal aid in the mediation process.

(3) The City Government provides legal aid to women and children victims of acts of Violence based on the provisions of laws and regulations.

(4) In providing legal aid as referred to in paragraph (3) the Regional Government may cooperate with legal aid institutions, women crisis centers, advocates and/or other legal assistants who are gender sensitive.

Article 35

The City Government makes efforts to improve family quality as referred to in Article 34 paragraph (1) letter a including:

a. guidance;

b. counseling;

c. supervision;

d. facilitation of family development implementation; and

e. improving the health quality of pregnant and breastfeeding mothers.

