c. social rehabilitation;

d. victim assistance, enforcement and legal aid; and

e. repatriation and social reintegration.

Article 24

(1) Counseling services as referred to in Article 23 paragraph (2) letter a aim to:

a. assist women and children victims of acts of violence in recognizing their problems and finding effective ways to overcome them themselves;

b. empower women and children victims of acts of Violence to determine their future;

c. provide moral support to women and children victims of acts of Violence in facing the process they are undergoing; and

d. make women and children victims of acts of Violence feel accepted in the community environment.

(2) Counseling services are carried out by social rehabilitation officers who are gender responsive, consisting of:

a. counselors;

b. psychologists; and

c. psychiatrists.

(3) Counseling services are carried out in a place that guarantees a sense of security, comfort and confidentiality of information from women and children victims of acts of Violence.

(4) The stages of counseling services include:

a. initial counseling;

b. follow-up counseling;

c. case discussion; and

d. site visits.

Part Two Complaint Handling

Article 20

Integrated services for complaint handling as referred to in Article 19 letter a are implemented by:

a. hospitals;

b. community health centers;

c. women and children service units;

d. trauma center protection houses;

e. women crisis centres;

f. advisory board for marriage guidance and preservation;

g. police;

h. prosecutor's office;

i. courts;

j. ministry of religious affairs; and/or

k. legal aid institutions.

Article 49

(1) Promotive efforts for basic health services as referred to in Article 48 paragraph (2) letter a are aimed at improving health status in a more optimal manner.

(2) Promotive efforts as referred to in paragraph (1) are carried out by providing socialization or outreach to the community regarding healthy lifestyles which include:

a. the importance of exclusive breastfeeding for infants;

b. the importance of immunization for toddlers;

c. clean living patterns;

d. the importance of nutritious food; and

e. the dangers of drugs and smoking.

(3) In addition to promotive efforts as referred to in paragraph (2), the City Government may carry out other activities in the context of health implementation.

CHAPTER VI CHILD PROTECTION

Article 64

The City Government, in order to provide protection to Children:

a. implements and supports national policies in the implementation of Child Protection in the City;

b. guarantees Children to exercise their rights in expressing opinions in accordance with the age and intelligence level of Children;

c. conducts prevention of Violence against Children by optimizing the role of customary institutions;

d. conducts socialization, seminars and counseling regarding Child Protection and rights;

e. develops child-friendly school models;

f. provides public facilities that are friendly and safe for Children; and

g. encourages public awareness of the importance of Protection for Children.

