d. spiritual mental guidance;

e. physical guidance;

f. social guidance and psychosocial counseling;

g. accessibility services;

h. social assistance and aid;

i. resocialization guidance;

j. follow-up guidance; and/or

k. referral.

(3) The provision of services as referred to in paragraph (2) is organized by:

a. Regional Apparatus that has duties and functions in the social field;

b. Regional Apparatus in charge of Women's and Children's Empowerment, and may cooperate with integrated service centers, women crisis centers or social community organizations that already exist; or c. the ministry of religion.

Article 69

(1) Parents in efforts to Protect Children's rights play a role in:

a. caring for, maintaining, educating, and protecting Children;

b. developing Children according to their abilities, talents, and interests;

c. providing formal and non-formal education;

d. providing character education and instilling moral values in Children;

e. guiding Children to behave in accordance with religion, ethics and morals as well as customs; and

f. preventing early marriage.

(2) In the event that parents are absent, or their whereabouts are unknown, or due to a certain reason, cannot carry out their obligations and responsibilities, then the obligations and responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1) may be transferred to the family.

Part Three Effectiveness of Services for Women and Children Victims of Acts of Violence

Part One General

Article 18

(1) Protection of women and children victims of acts of Violence is implemented through integrated services.

(2) The provision of integrated services for women and children victims of acts of Violence as referred to in paragraph (1) uses the principles of:

a. gender responsive;

b. non-discrimination;

c. equal and respectful relationships;

d. maintaining confidentiality;

e. providing a sense of security and comfort;

f. respecting individual differences;

g. non-judgmental;

h. respecting the choices and decisions of victims;

i. sensitive to the background and condition of victims and the use of language that is appropriate and understood by victims;

j. fast and simple;

k. empathy; and

l. fulfillment of the rights of women and children.

CHAPTER VIII GUIDANCE AND SUPERVISION

Article 70

(1) The City Government is obliged to conduct guidance and supervision of the implementation of Women's empowerment and protection of Child victims of Violence.

(2) Guidance as referred to in paragraph (1), includes:

a. guidelines and fulfillment standards;

b. technical guidance and training;

c. provision of facilities;

d. monitoring; and

e. evaluation.

(3) Guidance as referred to in paragraph (2) is in order to realize the objectives of empowerment and Protection of women and Child victims of Violence in accordance with minimum service standards.

Part Two Complaint Handling

Article 20

Integrated services for complaint handling as referred to in Article 19 letter a are implemented by:

a. hospitals;

b. community health centers;

c. women and children service units;

d. trauma center protection houses;

e. women crisis centres;

f. advisory board for marriage guidance and preservation;

g. police;

h. prosecutor's office;

i. courts;

j. ministry of religious affairs; and/or

k. legal aid institutions.

Article 4

The scope of regulation of Women's Empowerment and Child Protection includes:

a. protection of women;

b. protection of Children;

c. fulfillment of Children's rights;

d. improvement of quality of life of women and children;

e. community participation; and

f. guidance, supervision, assistance.

g. gender and child data system, disaggregated data on women and children;

Article 62

(1) In order to ensure the effectiveness of implementing the CFC policy in the Region, a CFC Task Force shall be established.

(2) The establishment and membership composition of the CFC Task Force shall be stipulated by Mayor Decree.

(3) The CFC Task Force shall be domiciled at the DPPKBPPPA Office.

(4) The CFC Task Force as referred to in paragraph (1) shall have the principal duties of:

a. coordinating the implementation of policies and development of CFC;

b. conducting socialization, advocacy and communication, information and education on CFC policies;

c. collecting baseline data for CFC development;

d. conducting needs analysis sourced from baseline data;

e. disseminating baseline data;

f. determining the focus and priority of programs in realizing CFC, adjusted to regional potential;

g. preparing a 5 (five) year Child-Friendly City regional action plan and work mechanism; and

h. conducting monitoring, evaluation and reporting at least once (1) a year.

