b. conducting socialization and advocacy of Women's Empowerment and Child Protection to sub-district heads, Village Heads and Village Chiefs;

c. preparing work programs every year;

d. encouraging the realization of planning and budgeting that is Responsive to Women's Empowerment and Child Protection;

e. preparing the work plan of the Women's Empowerment and Child Protection working group every year;

f. being accountable to the Mayor through the Deputy Mayor;

g. formulating policy recommendations to the Mayor;

h. preparing the profile of Regional Women's Empowerment and Child Protection;

i. monitoring the implementation of Women's Empowerment and Child Protection in each agency;

j. establishing a technical team to conduct analysis of the Regional budget;

k. preparing the City Women's Empowerment and Child Protection regional action plan; and

I. encouraging the implementation of selection and appointment of Focal Points in each Regional Apparatus.

Article 50

(1) Preventive efforts for basic health services as referred to in Article 48 paragraph (2) letter a are aimed at preventing the occurrence of disease or health problems.

(2) Efforts as referred to in paragraph (1) are carried out by:

a. requiring mothers to provide exclusive breastfeeding to infants up to 6 (six) months of age;

b. providing free immunization and vitamins to infants and toddlers;

c. providing nutritious food to toddlers from underprivileged families;

d. optimizing the role of posyandu for health services for mothers and toddlers;

e. conducting periodic health examinations; and

f. providing sanitation facilities and clean water in every school.

5.8.text:
CHAPTER III PROTECTION OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN

Part One General

Article 16

(1) The City Government in its efforts to Protect women and children is responsible for:

a. establishing and implementing policies, programs and activities for the protection of women and children;

b. conducting prevention efforts against acts of Violence against women and children including criminal acts of trafficking in persons;

c. increasing the effectiveness of services for women and children victims of acts of Violence;

d. increasing protection efforts for female workers;

e. strengthening and developing institutions providing Protection services for women and children;

f. conducting cooperation with the Government, other Regional Governments, private parties, business community and/or NGOs in efforts to prevent acts of Violence against women and children; and

g. involving the active role of families, customary leaders, religious leaders, communities, and institutions in the field of Protection of women and children.

(2) The responsibility of the City Government in efforts to Protect women as referred to in paragraph (1) is implemented by Regional Apparatus in accordance with their duties and functions and may coordinate with relevant agencies.

CHAPTER V PROTECTION AND FULFILLMENT OF CHILDREN'S RIGHTS

Part One

General

Article 42

(1) The City Government in efforts to protect and fulfill Children's rights is responsible for:

a. establishing and implementing policies and programs/activities for the protection and fulfillment of Children's rights;

b. respecting and guaranteeing the human rights of every Child without distinguishing ethnicity, religion, race, class, gender, ethnicity, culture and language, legal status of the Child, birth order of the Child, and physical and/or mental condition;

c. strengthening and developing institutions providing family quality improvement services in realizing Children's rights;

d. guaranteeing the Protection, care, and welfare of Children with due regard to the rights and obligations of parents, guardians, or other persons legally responsible for the Child;

e. providing facilities and infrastructure in the implementation of fulfillment of Children's rights;

f. guaranteeing the right of Children to express opinions in accordance with the age and level of intelligence of the Child;

g. conducting supervision and guidance in the context of implementation; and

h. cooperating with private parties in the implementation of Child Protection.

(2) The City Government's responsibility in efforts to protect Children as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be implemented by Regional Apparatus in accordance with their duties and functions.

Article 41

(1) In addition to the City Government, private parties providing Public services are required to provide lactation rooms.

(2) Lactation rooms as referred to in paragraph (1) constitute proper and adequate rooms.

(3) Private parties that do not provide lactation rooms as referred to in paragraph (2) shall be subject to administrative sanctions in the form of:

a. written warning;

b. limitation of business activities;

c. suspension of business activities;

d. administrative fines; and/or

e. revocation of permit.

CHAPTER VI CHILD PROTECTION

Article 64

The City Government, in order to provide protection to Children:

a. implements and supports national policies in the implementation of Child Protection in the City;

b. guarantees Children to exercise their rights in expressing opinions in accordance with the age and intelligence level of Children;

c. conducts prevention of Violence against Children by optimizing the role of customary institutions;

d. conducts socialization, seminars and counseling regarding Child Protection and rights;

e. develops child-friendly school models;

f. provides public facilities that are friendly and safe for Children; and

g. encourages public awareness of the importance of Protection for Children.

Article 53

(1) The implementation of social welfare is directed to:

a. individuals;

b. families;

c. groups; and/or

d. communities.

(2) The implementation of social welfare as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be prioritized for those who have lives that are not humanely decent and have social problem criteria:

a. poverty;

b. neglect;

c. disability;

d. remoteness;

e. social deviance and behavioral deviation;

f. disaster victims; and/or

g. victims of violence, exploitation and discrimination.

(3) Social welfare as referred to in paragraph (1) may be in the form of provision of services:

a. health;

b. education;

c. legal aid;

d. assistance;

e. social, mental and spiritual guidance;

f. social rehabilitation; and/or

g. reintegration.

Part Three Effectiveness of Services for Women and Children Victims of Acts of Violence

Part One General

Article 18

(1) Protection of women and children victims of acts of Violence is implemented through integrated services.

(2) The provision of integrated services for women and children victims of acts of Violence as referred to in paragraph (1) uses the principles of:

a. gender responsive;

b. non-discrimination;

c. equal and respectful relationships;

d. maintaining confidentiality;

e. providing a sense of security and comfort;

f. respecting individual differences;

g. non-judgmental;

h. respecting the choices and decisions of victims;

i. sensitive to the background and condition of victims and the use of language that is appropriate and understood by victims;

j. fast and simple;

k. empathy; and

l. fulfillment of the rights of women and children.

Part Four Social Rehabilitation

Article 23

(1) Social rehabilitation as referred to in Article 19 letter c is intended to restore and develop the capacity of women and children victims of acts of Violence who experience social dysfunction to be able to carry out their social functions in society properly.

(2) Social rehabilitation as referred to in paragraph (1) includes services:

a. motivation and psychosocial diagnosis;

b. care and nurturing;

c. vocational training and entrepreneurship development;

d. spiritual mental guidance;

e. physical guidance;

f. social guidance and psychosocial counseling;

g. accessibility services;

h. social assistance and aid;

i. resocialization guidance;

j. follow-up guidance; and/or

k. referral.

(3) The provision of services as referred to in paragraph (2) is organized by:

a. Regional Apparatus that has duties and functions in the social field;

b. Regional Apparatus in charge of Women's and Children's Empowerment, and may cooperate with integrated service centers, women crisis centers or social community organizations that already exist; or c. the ministry of religion.

MAYOR OF PRABUMULIH

PROVINCE OF SOUTH SUMATRA REGIONAL REGULATION OF PRABUMULIH CITY NUMBER 3 OF 2021

WOMEN'S EMPOWERMENT AND CHILD PROTECTION

BY THE GRACE OF GOD ALMIGHTY

MAYOR OF PRABUMULIH.

Considering : a. that to guarantee Women's Empowerment and Child Protection in accordance with their dignity and to guarantee their right to life in accordance with their nature and disposition without discrimination and to receive Protection from Violence and other violations, legal certainty is necessary;

b. that to provide legal certainty in the implementation of Women's Empowerment and Child Protection as well as to carry out the mandatory affairs of the Regional government, regulation in the form of a Regional regulation is necessary;

c. that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a, and letter b, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation on Women's Empowerment and Child Protection;

In View of : 1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;

2. Law Number 39 of 1999 concerning Human Rights (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1999 Number 165, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic Number 3886);

3. Law Number 6 of 2001 concerning the Establishment of Prabumulih City (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2001 Number 86, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4113);

4. Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2002 Number 109, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4235) as amended by Law Number 35 of 2014 concerning Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning

Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 297, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5606);

5. Law Number 11 of 2012 concerning the Juvenile Criminal Justice System (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2012 Number 153, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5332);

6. Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 244, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5587) as amended several times, most recently by Law Number 9 of 2015 concerning the Second Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2015 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5679);

7. Law Number 11 of 2020 concerning Job Creation (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2020 Number 245, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 6573);

8. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 1 of 2010 Concerning Minimum Service Standards in the Field of Integrated Services for Women and Children Victims of Violence (State News of the Republic of Indonesia of 2010 Number 56);

9. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 11 of 2011 Concerning Policy for the Development of Child-Friendly Regencies/Cities (State News of the Republic of Indonesia of 2012 Number 168);

10. Regulation of the Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 4 of 2018 concerning Guidelines for the Establishment of Regional Technical Implementation Units for Women and Child Protection (State News of the Republic of Indonesia of 2018 Number 532).

Article 3

This Regional Regulation aims to:

a. ensure the fulfillment of women's empowerment and child protection in the City;

b. serve as a guideline in planning, policy and strategy for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection;

c. protect and prevent women and Children from actions, decisions and policies that violate the rights of women and Children;

d. enhance the role and value of local wisdom as well as the role of customs in Women's Empowerment and Child Protection; and

e. serve as a legal umbrella for Regional Apparatus within the Prabumulih City Government that handles women's empowerment and child protection in order to provide assistance and support to women and child victims starting from investigation at the police, prosecution to trial in court.

Article 28

(1) Law enforcement and legal aid services are intended to provide assistance and Protection to women and children victims of acts of Violence and witnesses with a gender responsive approach so that victims obtain justice and legal certainty.

(2) Types of legal aid services include:

a. legal aid in the reporting process and preparation of investigation reports;

b. legal aid in the prosecution process at the prosecutor's office;

c. legal aid in the examination process at court;

d. legal aid in the court hearing decision process until there is a final legal decision; and

e. legal aid in the mediation process.

(3) The City Government provides legal aid to women and children victims of acts of Violence based on the provisions of laws and regulations.

(4) In providing legal aid as referred to in paragraph (3) the Regional Government may cooperate with legal aid institutions, women crisis centers, advocates and/or other legal assistants who are gender sensitive.

